[dvystrcil/obico] Add mid-print failure notification to Obico integration  (model=qwen3-coder-next:latest, 29s, lane=claude)
## Why
Obico currently monitors print status via Moonraker/OctoPrint APIs, but does not notify when a print fails mid-way. A mid-print failure (e.g., filament runout, nozzle jam, power loss) leaves the print incomplete without any alert, requiring manual inspection or post-run checks. This is a reliability gap for unattended printing.

## What we know
- The Obico integration is deployed via dvystrcil/obico Kubernetes manifests (base/server-deployment.yaml, base/tasks-deployment.yaml). It connects to Moonraker/OctoPrint APIs to fetch print status.
- The obico-docker repo builds images from upstream `TheSpaghettiDetective/obico-server` with local patches (obico-docker/.github/workflows/build.yaml).
- No existing issue in dvystrcil/obico or dvystrcil/homelab explicitly tracks mid-print failure alerts.
- Context from homelab/architecture/secret-scanning.md and homelab/issues/38 confirms cluster-resident MCP services are expected to expose status + audit + queue control, but alerting on failure states is not yet implemented.

## Acceptance criteria
- [ ] **AC1.** When a print transitions from "printing" to a terminal failure state (e.g., error, cancelled, failed) within 60 seconds, Obico sends an email or push notification (via configured SMTP or Obico cloud webhook) to the user.
- [ ] **AC2.** The notification includes the print job name, estimated completion time at failure, and a link to the Obico UI job details page.
- [ ] **AC3.** Mid-print failure notifications do not fire for intentional cancellations initiated by the user (i.e., distinguish between user-initiated cancel and system/device errors).

## Lane
claude — requires design decisions: which failure states qualify, how to detect them reliably from Moonraker/OctoPrint APIs, and whether to use Obico's built-in notification channels or integrate with a homelab-wide alerting system (e.g., Prometheus Alertmanager + n8n).

